in this episode of cloud performance  analyst I take a look at the right way  to profile a CDN will our latency hit  the mark stay tuned to find out in a  previous episode we talked about how the  Google cloud load balancer can be  supercharged to leverage CDN  capabilities for faster content  distribution to your users which of  course kicked off a discussion with  multiple developers about the nuances of  CDN performance and how Google's stacked  up now the minimum functionality for a  CDN is caching a requested asset closer  to users so that future requests have  the lowest possible latency to see what  this looks like performance wise we  simply set up an asset on the CDN and  fetched it via a curl command about 500  times or so you know things are working  properly when your graph looks something  like this you see a bunch of slower  requests at the front of your timeline  and then latency dropped significantly  once the caching occurs if you run the  same test but from various instances  distributed globally you'll get  something that looks like this we placed  an asset on a server in Texas and  fetched it from various points around  the world you can see how Google's edge  Network properly cached the asset and  moved it closer to the user for later  fetches now oh by the way if you're  super interested you can modify your  curl command to spit out all the sub  sections of those requests and graph  those individually things like DNS  resolve time connection and pre transfer  speeds can vary between CDN providers so  it's sometimes nice to see how these  things break out so that you can compare  them properly now sadly these tests show  a rough estimate of performance but to  really show off the power of Google CDN  you need to write a bigger test for  example the cloud team built a test  using Vegeta running on Google container  engine which allowed each pod to scale  up to about 50,000 RPS and an even  bigger test has been done by the  Sodexo's radar community which collects  over 14 billion real user measurements  each day from users all around the world  across providers which means that you  can compare them and see which one works  for your situation if you're interested  in more information on either of those  tests check out the article for this  video linked in the description below  where you can also find more great ideas  on how to optimize your cloud  performance because when it comes to  performance every millisecond counts
 

